# Keep the Netlify subdomain out of the index; the apex domain is canonical.
https://compoundingjourney.netlify.app/ https://compoundingjourney.com/ 301!
https://compoundingjourney.netlify.app/* https://compoundingjourney.com/:splat 301!

# The home page used to serve all three translations from one document and pick
# one with JavaScript, so "/", "/?lang=en" and "/?lang=pt" were the same bytes
# under three addresses. Each language now has its own pre-rendered page, and
# the query-string addresses are folded into it so their history is not lost.
# `/?lang=en` and `/?lang=pt` are handled by the language-preference edge
# function instead. The redirect engine forwards every query parameter it
# matched on to the destination, so these two rules sent /?lang=en to
# /en/?lang=en - a second 200 URL for the English home page, carrying a
# parameter that means nothing to the page that received it. The syntax has no
# way to drop a parameter, and the edge function already runs on this exact
# path for language negotiation, so doing it there costs no extra request.

# Spanish lives at the apex URL. /es/ is the address people guess from the
# other two, so it points there rather than 404ing.
/es/ / 301

# The journal used to render every article from one client-side shell, so each
# translation shared a URL, a canonical and an empty document. Those addresses
# now point at the pre-rendered page for the requested slug.
# The `post=` form is handled by the legacy-article-url edge function, for the
# same reason as the language parameter above: matching on `post` and then
# forwarding it produced /en/blog/slow-money-system/?post=slow-money-system,
# which is a duplicate of the article for every one of the fifteen articles.
#
# The bare form stays here. It is a plain path-to-path redirect with no
# parameter to lose, and keeping it in the redirect engine means these URLs
# still resolve if the edge function ever bypasses on error.
/en/blog/article.html /en/blog/ 301
/es/blog/article.html /es/blog/ 301
/pt/blog/article.html /pt/blog/ 301

# One page, one URL.
#
# Netlify serves a directory and the index.html inside it as two separate 200s,
# and serves a .html file with and without its extension as two more. Nothing
# links to the second form of either, but Google had found 36 of them - exactly
# as many as the site has real pages - and was spending half its crawl budget
# confirming that each one is a copy of a page it already had. The canonical
# tags were right, which is why none of this ever surfaced as an error; it
# surfaced as "alternate page with proper canonical tag" against half the site
# and as pages that never got crawled often enough to be indexed at all.
#
# These are forced. Without the `!` a rule loses to a file that exists at the
# same path, which is every path listed here.
#
# Spanish is first because its home page is the apex rather than /es/. Left to
# the language rule below it, /es/index.html would redirect to /es/ and then be
# redirected a second time by the /es/ rule at the top of this file; a chain is
# a slower crawl and one more hop for the equity to leak through.
/es/index.html / 301!
/index.html / 301!
/:lang/index.html /:lang/ 301!
/:lang/blog/index.html /:lang/blog/ 301!
/:lang/blog/:slug/index.html /:lang/blog/:slug/ 301!

# The same twin, for the page families the generators added: the calculators,
# the templates, the glossary, the sessions page and the three legal pages. They
# are matched with placeholders rather than written out because the section
# segment is localised - /es/calculadoras/, /en/calculators/, /pt/calculadoras/ -
# and listing every combination would be twenty-odd lines that have to be
# extended by hand every time a section is added. The first rule covers a
# section's own index and any two-segment page; the second covers one page
# inside a section; the third covers a category archive, which is one level
# deeper because it lives under /blog/.
#
# The /blog/ rules above come first on purpose: the first match wins, and a
# journal article would otherwise be collapsed by the generic rule to the same
# destination by a longer route.
/:lang/:section/index.html /:lang/:section/ 301!
/:lang/:section/:slug/index.html /:lang/:section/:slug/ 301!
/:lang/blog/:section/:slug/index.html /:lang/blog/:section/:slug/ 301!

# The extensionless twin of every generated .html page. The canonical form keeps
# the extension because that is what the sitemap, the hreflang clusters and the
# pages' own canonical tags have always declared - changing the canonical form
# would mean re-earning 15 URLs, while redirecting the twin costs nothing.
#
# The four tool slugs are written out rather than matched with a placeholder.
# A destination that concatenates a placeholder with a literal extension
# (/:lang/simulators/:slug.html) is not something the redirect syntax documents,
# and the failure mode if it is read as a placeholder named "slug.html" is all
# twelve tool pages redirecting to a 404.
/:lang/simulator /:lang/simulator.html 301!
/:lang/simulators/freedom-calendar /:lang/simulators/freedom-calendar.html 301!
/:lang/simulators/market-time-machine /:lang/simulators/market-time-machine.html 301!
/:lang/simulators/monte-carlo-fire /:lang/simulators/monte-carlo-fire.html 301!
/:lang/simulators/passive-income-engine /:lang/simulators/passive-income-engine.html 301!

# Navigation shortcuts. These used to rewrite to index.html with a 200, which
# served six byte-identical copies of the home page under different URLs.
#
# They point at /en/ rather than the apex. Every one of them is an English word,
# so the reader following one is reading English, and the apex is the Spanish
# site - sending them there handed them a page in a language they did not ask
# for and left the language switcher as the thing they had to find first. The
# fragments stay Spanish because the section ids are the same on all three
# published pages; only the document changes.
/pillars /en/#pilares 301
# /simulators used to land on the home page's simulator section, because that
# section was the closest thing the site had to a list of them. It now has one.
/simulators /en/simulators/ 301
/tools /en/#herramientas 301
/templates /en/#plantillas 301
/faq /en/#preguntas-frecuentes 301
/assessment /en/#assessment 301

# Build input, published by accident of geography.
#
# This site's publish directory is the repository root, so everything committed
# here that Netlify does not exclude is served. It excludes less than you would
# hope: node_modules and dotfiles are dropped, and netlify.toml with them, but
# package.json, package-lock.json, tailwind.config.js and the whole of scripts/
# were all fetchable at their repository paths. None of them is a secret - this
# is a static site and its build has no credentials in it - but a manifest of
# exact dependency versions is a shopping list for anyone looking for a known
# vulnerability, and the generators are not part of what the site serves.
#
# content/home and content/simulators are here for a different reason: they are
# the templates the published pages are generated from, so they were serving a
# second, unstyled, unversioned copy of the homepage and of every simulator at a
# URL nothing links. A noindex header kept them out of search results but still
# answered anyone who asked.
#
# content/blog is deliberately absent from this list. The per-language catalogs
# under it are fetched by assets/js/blog-index.js at runtime; blocking them
# would empty the journal index.
/scripts/* /404.html 404
/package.json /404.html 404
/package-lock.json /404.html 404
/tailwind.config.js /404.html 404
/content/home/* /404.html 404
/content/simulators/* /404.html 404
